-- title:
--   Joint injectivity of polar coefficients on section pairs in general position
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, $N$ a positive integer and $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, the subextension of the Laurent series field over $k$ generated by the $q$-expansions of $j$ and of $j$ at $q^N$; let $g$ be a semilinear automorphism of $F$ over $k$, i.e. a pair consisting of a ring automorphism of $F$ and one of $k$ compatible with the structure map. Let $W, E_1, E_2$ be finite sets of places of $F/k$ (valuation subrings of $F$ containing $k$, proper, with principal ideals), and assume: (hgp₁) every $h \in F$ with $\mathrm{ord}_v h \ge 0$ for $v \notin E_1$, $\mathrm{ord}_v h \ge -1$ for $v \in E_1$, and residue $0$ at every $w \in W$, vanishes; (hgp₂) every $h$ with $\mathrm{ord}_v h \ge 0$ off $E_2$ and $\ge -1$ on $E_2$ lies in $k$. Let $t$ assign to each place an element of $F$ with $\mathrm{ord}_v(t_v) = 1$ for $v \in E_1 \cup E_2$, all such $v$ having residue field generated by $k$ (surjective structure map to the residue field). Let $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $b_j = (b_{j,1}, b_{j,2})$, $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n$, satisfy $\mathrm{ord}_v b_{j,1} \ge 0$ off $E_1$ and $\ge -m$ on $E_1$, likewise $b_{j,2}$ with respect to $E_2$; for every $w \in W$ there is $c \in k$ with $b_{j,1}$ having residue $c$ at $w$ and $b_{j,2}$ having residue $c$ at $g \cdot w$; and the $b_j$ are independent modulo diagonal constants, i.e. $\sum_j c_j b_{j,1} = a = \sum_j c_j b_{j,2}$ with $a \in k$ forces $c = 0$. Then for $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to k$: if $\sum_j c_j \cdot a_r(b_{j,1} t_v^m; v, t_v) = 0$ for all $v \in E_1$ and all $r$ with $r + 1 < m$, and likewise $\sum_j c_j \cdot a_r(b_{j,2} t_v^m; v, t_v) = 0$ for all $v \in E_2$ and $r + 1 < m$, where $a_r(\,\cdot\,; v, t)$ denotes the $r$-th Taylor coefficient at $v$ along $t$, then $c = 0$.
--
--   This is the matrix form of the general-position statement for the glued fibre: the functionals "polar coefficient of order $r - m$ at $v$", for $v \in E_1 \sqcup E_2$ and $r + 1 < m$, are jointly injective on the $k$-span of the pairs $b_j$, so that when their number matches $n$ the corresponding square matrix is invertible. It is used in the proof that the Jacobian determinant at the centre of a prolongation tuple is a unit, [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.IsModel.isUnit_det_jacobian_centre_of_mDivRepresents`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.IsModel.isUnit_det_jacobian_centre_of_mDivRepresents); the kernel form it rests on is [`ModularCurve.exists_eq_algebraMap_of_forall_taylorCoeff_mul_pow_eq_zero_of_generalPosition`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_eq_algebraMap_of_forall_taylorCoeff_mul_pow_eq_zero_of_generalPosition), together with additivity and $k$-homogeneity of Taylor coefficients.
